# Copy to .env.local for development; set the same names in Vercel for production.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHO MAY OPEN THIS SITE AT ALL. Read by proxy.ts, which runs before every
# request - pages, /api, static files and the agent installer alike.
#
# Comma separated. Single addresses and CIDR ranges, IPv4 and IPv6:
#   ALLOWED_IPS=39.34.163.45,203.0.113.0/24,2a02:1234:5678::/48
#
# These are PUBLIC IPs - the address your ISP gave the router. A 192.168.x.x is
# useless here: it is internal to your own network and Vercel never sees it.
# Every PC behind one router shares one public IP, so one entry covers a whole
# office; a second office or a second internet line needs its own entry.
#
# To find an entry: connect a PC to that wifi, open the site, and copy the
# address the "Access restricted" page prints. That is the exact string
# proxy.ts compares against, which a "what is my IP" site cannot promise.
#
# LEFT EMPTY, EVERYTHING IS BLOCKED - deliberately, so a forgotten gate fails
# shut rather than open. Localhost is always allowed so `npm run dev` works.
# Next inlines this at build time: changing it in Vercel needs a redeploy.
ALLOWED_IPS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SERVER-ONLY. No NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, and that is the whole point: anything
# prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_ is compiled into the JavaScript the browser downloads
# and is readable in DevTools. These two are read only inside
# app/api/plan/route.ts, which runs on the server.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The planning half - Excel in, plan out. Cloud Run in production.
# Leave it on localhost while the local backend is still playing that role.
# CLOUD_API=http://localhost:8000

# # Must match CLOUD_API_KEY in the backend's environment. Leave EMPTY for local
# # development, where the backend runs without a key.
# # Generate one with:  python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
# CLOUD_API_KEY=

# # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# # PUBLIC, deliberately. This one reaches the browser, and must - it is the
# # address the browser itself dials. It is not a secret: it is localhost.
# # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# # The rendering half, on the machine the browser is running on. This is
# # localhost BY DEFINITION - it is not a server you deploy, it is the designer's
# # own PC. Only change the port if the agent was configured to use another one.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AGENT_URL=http://localhost:8765
